# Tables

@lede
Tables open with `@t`. The first `|` row is the header. There is no
separator row.
@end

## The basic form

@t
| Name | Age | City
| John | 30 | NYC
| Jane | 25 | LA

The source for that:

```brief
@t
| Name | Age | City
| John | 30 | NYC
| Jane | 25 | LA
```

The trailing `|` is optional. Cells are trimmed of leading and trailing
whitespace.

## Strict rules

- Every row must have the same number of cells as the header. A mismatch
  is @ec[B0502].
- The table ends at the first line that does not begin with `|`, or at
  end of file.
- Inline content inside a cell is parsed as inline — emphasis, inline
  code, and inline shortcodes work.

## Column alignment

Alignment is set on the directive line as an array argument:

@t(align: [left, right, center])
| Name | Score | Status
| Ada | 1024 | active
| Bob | 42 | idle

Source:

```brief
@t(align: [left, right, center])
| Name | Score | Status
| Ada | 1024 | active
| Bob | 42 | idle
```

The alignment array length must match the column count. A mismatch is
@ec[B0504] *"alignment array length must match column count"*.

Allowed values: `left`, `right`, `center`.

## Why no separator row

Markdown's `| --- | :---: |` separator carries one piece of information —
column alignment — but visually duplicates the header structure. Brief
moves alignment to the directive (`align: [...]`), drops the separator
row entirely, and saves both source tokens and LLM-mode tokens.

## In LLM mode

Tables are emitted in compact pipe form with one space of padding around
each cell. The header row is followed by a single line `|---|` *only* if
`--keep-table-rule` is passed; otherwise no separator is emitted.

## What `@t` is not

`@t` is a special-cased self-terminating block shortcode — the same form
as the triple-backtick code fence. No other shortcode (built-in or
user-defined) is permitted to be self-terminating. The grammar exception
exists for tables and code blocks alone, on the basis of source-level
brevity.
